1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,927
Every face in this 4K street was found
by an AI model running on my laptop's

2
00:00:04,927 --> 00:00:08,510
GPU, inside a C# app, and not
a single frame left the PC.

3
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:13,083
Let me show you how I plugged that AI
in, how I made it fast, and how the app

4
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:15,650
learns which faces belong
to the same person.

5
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:20,150
The first choice is the model, and
there's no single right answer.

6
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:25,556
On this frame, a small model, UltraFace,
finds two faces, a balanced one, YOLO11

7
00:00:25,556 --> 00:00:29,410
nano, finds six, and a large
one, YOLO26, finds twenty-two.

8
00:00:29,590 --> 00:00:33,579
The price is time: about three
milliseconds a frame, then five, then

9
00:00:33,579 --> 00:00:37,510
seventy-five, plus a gigabyte of
graphics memory for the large one.

10
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:41,330
More boxes doesn't mean more correct,
so I'm not picking a winner.

11
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:46,761
And models keep improving: by its makers'
own numbers, the newest small YOLO is

12
00:00:46,761 --> 00:00:50,550
about thirty percent faster
on a CPU than the one before.

13
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:55,158
So I built the app to let you choose the
trade-off today, and plug in a faster

14
00:00:55,158 --> 00:00:56,010
model tomorrow.

15
00:00:56,670 --> 00:01:01,707
The model runs through ONNX Runtime
inside the app, with no Python and no

16
00:01:01,707 --> 00:01:02,190
server.

17
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:07,173
The DirectML provider hands the work to
any DirectX 12 graphics card, not just

18
00:01:07,173 --> 00:01:11,852
NVIDIA, and if the GPU can't load a
model, the app falls back to the CPU and

19
00:01:11,852 --> 00:01:13,330
shows which one it used.

20
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:19,008
To find the faces in the same 1080p
frame, the CPU needs twenty-three

21
00:01:19,008 --> 00:01:24,497
milliseconds, the laptop's built-in
Intel graphics fourteen, and the RTX less

22
00:01:24,497 --> 00:01:25,210
than five.

23
00:01:25,390 --> 00:01:29,540
But at 4K there's a new limit: before
the AI can look at a frame, the decoder

24
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:33,744
has to pull it out of the compressed
video, and that takes thirteen to sixteen

25
00:01:33,744 --> 00:01:36,870
milliseconds, longer than the
GPU needs to find the faces.

26
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:40,730
So the decoder is now the
bottleneck, and it sets the pace.

27
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:43,900
Two more tricks make the scan fast.

28
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:48,771
First, before the model can look at a
frame, the app copies it into the model's

29
00:01:48,771 --> 00:01:53,046
input: a square grid of numbers, 640
on each side, that takes about five

30
00:01:53,046 --> 00:01:53,640
megabytes.

31
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:57,863
Making a new buffer for every frame
kept filling the memory, so the garbage

32
00:01:57,863 --> 00:02:02,175
collector had to stop the whole app
ninety-nine times in three hundred frames to

33
00:02:02,175 --> 00:02:02,660
clean up.

34
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:07,960
Now the app reuses one buffer for
every frame, and those pauses are gone.

35
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:12,661
Second, scanning every frame is wasted
work, because at sixty frames a second

36
00:02:12,661 --> 00:02:15,420
very little changes from
one frame to the next.

37
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,947
So the app scans five frames a second:
frame zero, twelve, twenty-four,

38
00:02:19,947 --> 00:02:23,560
thirty-six and forty-eight,
and skips the other fifty-five.

39
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:27,497
With the large model at about seventy-five
milliseconds per frame, scanning all

40
00:02:27,497 --> 00:02:30,968
sixty would take four and a half
seconds for every second of video, while

41
00:02:30,968 --> 00:02:33,060
scanning five takes less
than half a second.

42
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:37,492
Those other frames still have to be
decoded, because a video stores most frames

43
00:02:37,492 --> 00:02:41,743
as changes from the ones before, but
OpenCV never turns them into color pixels,

44
00:02:41,743 --> 00:02:45,080
and that alone made reading the
video more than twice as fast.

45
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:49,460
The catch is that boxes now
exist only on the scanned frames.

46
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:51,560
So what happens to a face in between?

47
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:56,769
If the box just stays where the last scan
saw it, a moving face walks right out

48
00:02:56,769 --> 00:02:58,950
of its blur, and that's a privacy leak.

49
00:02:59,130 --> 00:03:04,202
The fix is to interpolate: slide each box
toward where the same face shows up at

50
00:03:04,202 --> 00:03:05,090
the next scan.

51
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:10,445
But that only works if the app knows
which box on the next scan is the same

52
00:03:10,445 --> 00:03:10,790
face.

53
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:13,120
That's the tracker's job.

54
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:18,479
It predicts where each face is heading
with a constant-velocity model, and joins

55
00:03:18,479 --> 00:03:21,780
each new box to the track
it overlaps most, by IoU.

56
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:26,863
When a small face moves too fast to
overlap, a size-gated distance match still

57
00:03:26,863 --> 00:03:29,880
joins it, if it's close
and about the same size.

58
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:34,375
On one minute of footage, that cut the
number of tracks by more than two thirds,

59
00:03:34,375 --> 00:03:38,420
and with interpolation, boxes visibly
off the face became four times rarer.

60
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:43,453
But a track ends whenever someone leaves
the screen, so even a short film gives

61
00:03:43,453 --> 00:03:47,420
you over a hundred of them, and nobody
wants to review a hundred separate

62
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:47,800
tracks.

63
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:51,420
So for each track, the app
picks three good shots.

64
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:56,265
YuNet finds five landmarks, the eyes, the
nose and the corners of the mouth, the

65
00:03:56,265 --> 00:04:00,639
face is aligned, and ArcFace turns it
into an embedding of five hundred and

66
00:04:00,639 --> 00:04:02,680
twelve numbers, like a fingerprint.

67
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:07,096
Then tracks with similar fingerprints
are grouped into one suggested person,

68
00:04:07,096 --> 00:04:11,220
with one hard rule: two faces on the
same frame are never the same person.

69
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:15,120
On this film, a hundred and three
tracks became thirty-five people.

70
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,840
Now naming is fast.

71
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:22,448
I name Thom and Celia once, and as the
film plays, Celia leaves the screen and

72
00:04:22,448 --> 00:04:26,932
comes back in a new scene already labeled
Celia, because the app knows it's the

73
00:04:26,932 --> 00:04:27,500
same face.

74
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:32,509
Grouping can still miss, like this shot
of Thom in profile, so I merge it into

75
00:04:32,509 --> 00:04:35,480
Thom with one click, and
any edit can be undone.

76
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:39,809
And because a face fingerprint is
biometric data, grouping only runs if you

77
00:04:39,809 --> 00:04:43,460
allow it, and the fingerprints stay
in memory and are never saved.

78
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:48,813
So the AI finds the faces, the tracker
follows them, and grouping turns them

79
00:04:48,813 --> 00:04:50,690
into people, all on the laptop.

80
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:55,121
But a model can still miss a face, and
that's where the human comes in, in the

81
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:55,830
next episode.

